.TH b4-bookings-show 1  "b4 0.1.0" 
.SH NAME
b4\-bookings\-show \- Show every stored field of one booking
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBb4 bookings show\fR [\fB\-\-account\fR] [\fB\-\-db\fR] [\fB\-o\fR|\fB\-\-output\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-header\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] <\fIID\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Show every stored field of one booking
.PP
Show every stored field of one booking, including the ones `bookings list` has no room for: value date, SEPA references, mandate, notes and the complete purpose text. `b4 schema bookings` describes the fields.
.PP
An id is unique within one account only. If several accounts have a booking with the id, all of them are shown, each with its account; give \-\-account to pick one.
.PP
Table output leaves out fields without a value. With \-\-output json the result is one object with every field, or an array of such objects when several bookings have the id; jsonl prints one object and csv one row per booking.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-account\fR \fI<ACCOUNT>\fR
Only the booking of this account

An id is unique within one account only. ACCOUNT is tried in this order: the numeric id from `b4 accounts list`; the IBAN (blanks and case do not matter); the account number; a part of the account name (case does not matter) that only one account has, where a complete name wins over a part. If several accounts match, the command fails and lists them with their ids.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.TP
<\fIID\fR>
The id of the booking, as printed by `b4 bookings list`
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-db\fR \fI<PATH>\fR [default: ./b4.duckdb]
Database file: written by `decrypt`, read by every other command
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBB4_DB\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fI<FORMAT>\fR
Output format [default: table]

table is made for reading: line breaks, control characters and text direction overrides in the data are replaced. csv, jsonl and json are data: written to a file or a pipe they are exact, byte for byte. Only when they are written to a terminal are the characters a terminal acts on (escape sequences, text direction overrides) made harmless: csv shows U+FFFD in their place, json and jsonl write them as \\uXXXX escapes.

In json and jsonl a number that a double cannot hold exactly is a string of its digits: an integer beyond 2^53, a decimal of more than 15 significant digits.
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
table: Aligned text, fitted to the terminal
.IP \(bu 2
json: One JSON array of objects
.IP \(bu 2
jsonl: One JSON object per line
.IP \(bu 2
csv: Comma\-separated values with a header line
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-header\fR
Leave out the header line of table and csv output
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Do not print informational messages to stderr

Silences progress and summary lines and the "no rows" note. Warnings and errors are still printed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
b4 bookings show 796370400
    Show the booking with this id.
b4 bookings show 796370400 \-\-account giro
    Only the booking with this id in one account.
b4 bookings show 796370400 \-\-output json
    The same as a JSON object with every field.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fBb4\fR(1),
\fBb4\-bookings\fR(1)
